{
  "id": "colinhacks_zod_6452",
  "repo_name": "colinhacks/zod",
  "repo_url": "https://github.com/colinhacks/zod",
  "base_commit": "36c68adf6834ef6ff40bbcb5671371b33711ce84",
  "pr_number": 6452,
  "issue_number": 6204,
  "issue_title": "Add support for RFC3339 full-time in z.iso.time()",
  "issue_body": "## Problem\n\n`z.iso.time()` validates RFC3339 `partial-time`, explicitly rejecting timezone offsets.\n\nHowever, OpenAPI defines\n\n```yaml\ntype: string\nformat: time\n```\n\nas RFC3339 `full-time`. As a result, valid OpenAPI values like\n\n```text\n10:15:30Z\n10:15:30+02:00\n```\n\ncannot be validated using the built-in ISO validators.\n\n## Use case\n\nI'm using [Hey API](https://heyapi.dev/) to generate Zod schemas from an OpenAPI specification.\n\nFor most OpenAPI formats there is a corresponding Zod validator, but `format: time` currently has no equivalent because `z.iso.time()` implements `partial-time` instead of `full-time`.\n\nThis makes it impossible to generate a correct validator for an OpenAPI schema like:\n\n```yaml\ntype: string\nformat: time\n```\n\nwithout resorting to a custom regex or `.refine()`.\n\n## Possible solutions\n\nSome possible APIs could be:\n\n```ts\nz.iso.time({ offset: true })\n```\n\n(similar to `z.iso.datetime({ offset: true })`)\n\nor\n\n```ts\nz.iso.fullTime()\n\n```\n\n## Conclusion\n\nSince OpenAPI and Zod are commonly used together, it would be great if OpenAPI's standard format: time could be mapped directly to a built-in Zod validator without requiring custom validation logic.\n\nRelated to #5542 ",
  "merged_at": "2026-08-24T17:21:13Z",
